.TH CJSON_CLONE 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cjson_clone \- deep-copy a DOM subtree
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cjson.h>

cjson cjson_clone(cjson node);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns a fully independent copy of the subtree under node.  The
function duplicates every string.  It copies every array and every
object together with its contents.  The clone uses the same
allocator as the source, which is the allocator that the root node
keeps.  Each object of the copy keeps its members in the order of
the source object.

The function refuses a subtree that nests more than 500 levels deep.
Each array or object is one level, in any combination, and a scalar
adds none.  It reports such a
subtree in the same way as an allocation failure.  The limit is the
same as the limit of the parser, so the function can clone any tree
that parsed.  Only the construction API can build a deeper tree,
through cjson_list_push() and cjson_dictionary_set().  That API has
no depth bound, where a parsed document does.  The limit is a policy
limit.  It is not a guess about the stack size of the calling
thread.  The function walks the nesting with an explicit worklist on
the heap.  A tree at the limit therefore costs the same small, fixed
amount of native stack as a flat one.  An ordinary tree never comes
near this depth.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A new root node on success.  The caller owns it.  NULL when the
allocation fails, or when node nests more than 500 levels deep.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cjson_destroy (3),
.BR cjson_dictionary_first (3)
